{:edb/db-before-t 1333 :edb/db-after-t 1334 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534646 50 #inst "2026-10-12T05:51:10.738Z" 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186133392 1078 #edb/ref 17592186133394 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186133446 1078 #edb/ref 17592186133450 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186133686 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\np_1(t)=t+1, \\qquad\np_2(t)=t, \\qquad\np_3(t)=t-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ of polynomials of degree $1$ or less in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle =p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $p_1(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $p_2(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $S=\\{ p_1(t), p_2(t), p_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 false] [17592186133686 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\np_1(t)=t+1, \\qquad\np_2(t)=t, \\qquad\np_3(t)=t-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ of polynomials of degree $1$ or less in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle =p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $p_1(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. $p_2(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{ p_1(t), p_2(t), p_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186133687 1071 "![](images/c8/c817b41f0c393fcd49b34d4d7cb6ea6d4b9caf9aac7576c4c678090af3883c8f.png)\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_3$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $S=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 false] [17592186133687 1071 "![](images/d2/d28edbbb8741260e5087e4ecc1424d03208354580d8aff6e2baeae01ba8d2c97.svg)\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_3$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186133688 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\nf_1(t)=1, \\qquad\nf_2(t)=t, \\qquad\nf_3(t)=2t+1\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\int_{-1}^{1} f(t)g(t) \\: \\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f_1(t) \\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $f_2(t) \\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\n- $S=\\{ f_1(t), f_2(t), f_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 false] [17592186133688 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\nf_1(t)=1, \\qquad\nf_2(t)=t, \\qquad\nf_3(t)=2t+1\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\int_{-1}^{1} f(t)g(t) \\: \\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f_1(t) \\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. $f_2(t) \\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{ f_1(t), f_2(t), f_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186133870 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430807 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186133871 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430805 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186133871 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430806 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186133871 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430808 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186133991 1050 780.0 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186134055 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130486 13194139534646 17592186045417 false] [17592186134055 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t+1,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(-1)q(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 false] [17592186134055 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t+1,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(-1)q(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186134559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186134559 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-1,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t-2.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186134559 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n$p_{1}(t) = t, p_{2}(t) = t - 1, p_{3}(t) = 2t - 2$.\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $R_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as\n$⟨p(t), q(t)⟩ = p(0)q(0) + p(1)q(1)$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $p_{1}(t)⊥p_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $p_{1}(t)⊥p_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S = {p_{1}(t), p_{2}(t), p_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 false] [17592186134559 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,t-1⟩ \\\\  & =(t){|}_{0}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{0}+(t){|}_{1}\\cdot (t-1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (0-1)+1\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,2t-2⟩ \\\\  & =(t){|}_{0}\\cdot (2t-2){|}_{0}+(t){|}_{1}\\cdot (2t-2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =0\\cdot (2(0)-2)+1\\cdot (2(1)-2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,2t-2⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{0}\\cdot (2t-2){|}_{0}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (2t-2){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(0-1)\\cdot (2(0)-2)+(1-1)\\cdot (2(1)-2) \\\\  & =2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186134560 1071 "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 false] [17592186134560 1071 "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186134561 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 false] [17592186134561 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186134568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186134568 1071 "$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space ${R}^{3}$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+4{x}_{3}{y}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186134568 1071 "$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space $R^{3}$ is defined as\n$⟨x, y⟩ = x_{1}y_{1} + x_{2}y_{2} + 4x_{3}y_{3}$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v_{1}⊥v_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $v_{1}⊥v_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S = {v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 false] [17592186134568 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(4)(-8)+(-2)(4)+4(2)(5) \\\\  & =-32-8+40 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(4)(1)+(-2)(2)+4(2)(0) \\\\  & =4-4+0 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{1}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, computing the inner product $⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(-8)(1)+(4)(2)+4(5)(0) \\\\  & =-8+8+0 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${v}_{2}⊥{v}_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product.\nAs a result, the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186135104 1064 "Let's consider an inner product space $V$ equipped with the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle.$ \nA set of vectors \n\n$$\n{U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}\n$$\n\nis called an **orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product** if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set\n\n![](images/69/69571997d87ed3f40ee7e9b0742075fbd5ee107892f6a9b4198f75f3f5a9fce3.svg)\n\nand the inner product on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} \\rangle = x_1 y_1 + 3x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${V}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${V}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each inner product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(2)+3(1)(2)+2(1)(-4)=2+6-8=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(-3)+3(1)(1)+2(1)(0)=-3+3+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(2)(-3)+3(2)(1)+2(-4)(0)=-6+6+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${V}$ is an orthogonal set." 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186135104 1064 "Let's consider an inner product space $V$ equipped with the inner product $\\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle.$ \nA set of vectors \n\n$$\n{U}=\\{ \\mathbf{u}_1, \\cdots, \\mathbf{u}_p \\}\n$$\n\nis called an **orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product** if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand the inner product on $\\mathbb{R}^3$ defined as \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} \\rangle = x_1 y_1 + 3x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3.\n$$\n\nIf we denote the vectors in our set ${V}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${V}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩=0 \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each inner product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{2}⟩ & =(1)(2)+3(1)(2)+2(1)(-4)=2+6-8=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{1},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(1)(-3)+3(1)(1)+2(1)(0)=-3+3+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ ⟨{v}_{2},{v}_{3}⟩ & =(2)(-3)+3(2)(1)+2(-4)(0)=-6+6+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo indeed, ${V}$ is an orthogonal set." 13194139534646 17592186045417 false] [17592186237778 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=1-t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 false] [17592186237778 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=1-t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139534646 17592186045417 true] [17592186237780 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degr